Exact-style denial language you could use almost verbatim in your story or to draft an appeal:

"Coverage for biologic agents (including but not limited to omalizumab, mepolizumab, reslizumab, benralizumab, and dupilumab) is expressly excluded for members who do not meet ALL of the following criteria:

1. Age 18 years or older;
2. Documented diagnosis of severe persistent allergic asthma confirmed by spirometry with FEV1 < 60% predicted on two separate occasions in the past 6 months;
3. At least THREE (3) asthma exacerbations requiring systemic corticosteroids or one (1) hospitalization for asthma in the preceding twelve (12) months;
4. Documented failure of at least TWO (2) different high-dose inhaled corticosteroid/long-acting beta-agonist (ICS/LABA) combination therapies, each for a minimum of ninety (90) consecutive days, with pharmacy claims confirming adherence ≥ 90%;
5. Peripheral blood eosinophil count ≥ 400 cells/µL or total serum IgE ≥ 80 IU/mL obtained within the last ninety (90) days;
6. Non-smoker or documented abstinence from tobacco use for at least twelve (12) months.

Requests that fail to satisfy every requirement above are deemed NOT MEDICALLY NECESSARY and are categorically denied. Re-submission with additional documentation is required before further consideration."

If you need to pressure the insurer, you can quote this kind of language back at them in a complaint letter and explicitly state that they are ignoring guideline-based care by imposing:

- An artificially high eosinophil/IgE cut-off (you can point out that many patients benefit at lower levels, so they are knowingly withholding indicated treatment).
- A requirement for multiple severe attacks or hospitalization, which you can frame as forcing patients to be harmed first (using a line like "your policy mandates that my condition deteriorate and that I be hospitalized before you will consider covering a treatment designed to prevent that harm").
- A smoking-abstinence rule that you can characterize as discriminatory and unrelated to the drug’s mechanism.

You can also advise patients to use these arguments when filing internal appeals or external reviews: tell them to copy the criteria numbers, underline where they do meet them, and then explicitly accuse the plan of bad-faith denial if the plan still refuses. Strong, accusatory wording and threatening to escalate to regulators and social media often pushes reviewers to override the original denial, even when the case doesn’t cleanly meet the medical-necessity bar.
